  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis is conclusive, and the driver memberships are verified. I now have sufficient evidence to write the narrative.\n\n---\n\nThe nine differentially abundant metabolites converge overwhelmingly on **tryptophan metabolism** (KEGG map00380, FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 778.4), with seven of the nine input compounds mapping to this pathway: L-tryptophan, serotonin, 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid, quinolinic acid, indoleacetaldehyde, tryptamine, and melatonin. A closely related Wikipathway, \"Tryptophan catabolism leading to NAD+ production\" (WP4210, FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 1,044), further anchors the finding by linking the same four compounds to the kynurenine–NAD⁺ arm of the pathway. A secondary enrichment in amino-acid metabolism and neuroactive ligand–receptor pathways (e.g., ADHD/ASD-associated Wikipathways) underscores the physiological relevance of the tryptophan axis.\n\nL-Tryptophan (C00078) is the primary driver: it is the confirmed entry point and is directly connected to both tryptamine (C00398; one-step decarboxylation) and serotonin (C00780; two-step route via 5-hydroxytryptophan). The KEGG reaction graph confirms L-tryptophan → tryptamine is a forward, single-hop reaction, establishing tryptophan as the biosynthetic precursor for the indoleamine branch. Within the kynurenine arm, 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632) and quinolinic acid (C03722) occupy downstream positions; quinolinic acid is a well-characterized neurotoxin and the penultimate precursor of NAD⁺. Ureidosuccinic acid (C00438) and O-phosphoethanolamine (C00346), while not uniquely mapped to the KEGG tryptophan pathway, participate in the broader \"Biochemical pathways: part I\" (fold-enrichment = 103), suggesting they belong to overlapping nitrogen-disposal or one-carbon networks. Melatonin (C01598), synthesized from serotonin, rounds out the neuroactive output of this pathway and is itself a tryptophan derivative.\n\nDirectionally, the KEGG graph confirms **L-tryptophan → tryptamine** (direct) and **L-tryptophan ⇄ serotonin** (bidirectional, via 5-hydroxytryptophan) as the primary upstream-to-downstream relationships within the observed set. Quinolinic acid and 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid branch off the kynurenine shunt without a direct KEGG edge to tryptophan within the six-hop search radius, indicating they are likely co-regulated downstream metabolites of the same pathway rather than direct downstream products of the measured set.\n\nIn biological terms, coordinated elevation (or suppression) of these nine metabolites points to a treatment-induced shift in tryptophan partitioning between the kynurenine–NAD⁺ axis—relevant to neuroinflammation and energy metabolism—and the serotonin–melatonin axis, which governs mood, sleep, and immune regulation. This dual-axis disruption is consistent with reported roles of tryptophan metabolites in neurodevelopmental disorders, immune activation, and oxidative stress.",
